<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xanthos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xanthus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xanthos was the chief city of ancient Lycia, on the Xanthos (Eşen) river in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says its citizens destroyed themselves rather than surrender to the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> around 540 BCE, and the city later raised the pillar tombs of the Lycian dynasts, including the Inscribed Pillar with the longest Lycian text. With nearby Letoon it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xanthos.jpg" alt="Xanthos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xanthus,_tomb_of_the_Nereids,_podium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Xanthos, called Arna in Lycian, stood on a hill on the left bank of the Xanthos river near the present village of Kınık in Antalya Province. Its acropolis dates from the 8th century BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called it the largest city in Lycia. The sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a> at Letoon, 4 kilometres to the south, dates from the late 6th century BCE and was linked to the city by a sacred road. The city was a centre of culture and commerce for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a> and later for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> who conquered the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and Appian describe the conquest of Xanthos by the Median general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> for the Persian Empire. According to Herodotus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> beat a small Lycian army north of the city about 540 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a> retreated inside and were besieged. They destroyed their acropolis and killed their wives, children and slaves before a suicidal attack. Under Persian rule a local leadership was installed, coins were minted by 520 BCE and by 516 BCE the city appeared in the tribute list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>.</p><p>Representatives of the cities of the Lycian League submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> at Xanthos. Arrian describes a peaceful surrender, while Appian says the city was sacked. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> took it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>. In 42 BCE Brutus besieged and destroyed Xanthos after the Lycian League refused to contribute funds, and only 150 men survived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says the people set fire to their own city in a mass suicide. It was rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, and in the later Roman and Byzantine periods it gained luxury houses and several churches. Earthquakes in the sixth century damaged buildings, and the city was later destroyed and deserted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The number and quality of its surviving tombs are the site's notable feature. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nereid-monument">Nereid Monument</a> inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausoleum-at-halicarnassus">Mausoleum at Halicarnassus</a> in Caria. The Nereid Monument, the Tomb of Payava and the original sculptures of the Harpy Tomb are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, while the Harpy Tomb stands in its original place with replica reliefs. Excavations have produced inscriptions in Lycian and Greek, including bilingual texts that help in understanding Lycian, and the trilingual Xanthian Obelisk records the older language called Milyan. Xanthos and Letoon were declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1988.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xanthos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In c. 540 BCE the Persian general Harpagus conquered Lycia; Herodotus tells that the men of <b>Xanthos</b> killed their families and died fighting.</blockquote><a href="/state/lycian-dynasts">Lycian dynasts</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xanthos</b>, Patara, Pinara, Olympos, Myra and Tlos were among its six largest members.</blockquote><a href="/state/lycian-league">Lycian League</a></li></ul></section>
